WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.720
I want you to picture a piece of construction paper.

00:03.080 --> 00:05.880
Oh, yeah. Like the kind taped up on a refrigerator.

00:06.300 --> 00:10.120
Exactly. Maybe it was handed to you by, you know, a very proud kindergartner.

00:10.420 --> 00:10.740
Right.

00:11.200 --> 00:15.500
And on it is a drawing of a person. But, I mean, it's not just any person. It's this

00:15.500 --> 00:21.560
giant, wobbly circle. And sticking straight out of the bottom of this circle are two completely

00:21.560 --> 00:23.260
straight, rigid legs.

00:23.660 --> 00:25.120
The classic tadpole person.

00:25.120 --> 00:32.100
Yes, exactly. There is literally no torso. It is just a massive head with legs. Or maybe

00:32.100 --> 00:36.280
you're looking at a different drawing from that same kid and the human figure has these

00:36.280 --> 00:38.360
enormous paddle-like hands.

00:38.820 --> 00:41.380
Oh, with the fingers that look like thick bunches of bananas.

00:41.620 --> 00:46.760
Yes. Completely out of proportion to the rest of the, like, slender, tiny body. You've seen

00:46.760 --> 00:47.940
these drawings. I mean, we all have.

00:48.060 --> 00:49.060
We definitely have.

00:49.260 --> 00:54.360
And usually when we look at them, we just smile, right? We think they're cute and we subconsciously

00:54.360 --> 00:56.900
file them away in our brains as, well, mistakes.

00:57.360 --> 00:59.480
Right. Like, they just don't know any better yet.

00:59.760 --> 01:05.780
Exactly. We view them as this clumsy, adorable evidence of poor motor skills. Or just a developing

01:05.780 --> 01:11.160
brain that hasn't quite figured out how to accurately capture reality yet. But, um, what

01:11.160 --> 01:13.480
if we've been looking at this entirely backwards?

01:14.020 --> 01:15.740
Which is such a wild thing to consider.

01:16.240 --> 01:17.020
It really is.

01:17.080 --> 01:21.560
What if those banana hands and those giant head bodies aren't errors of perception at all?

01:21.560 --> 01:26.480
What if they are actually evidence of a highly organized, brilliant, and incredibly strict

01:26.480 --> 01:27.620
cognitive achievement?

01:28.020 --> 01:32.740
That is the fundamental shift in perspective we are taking today. Because the moment you

01:32.740 --> 01:37.440
stop looking at a child's drawing as, you know, a defective picture just waiting for adult

01:37.440 --> 01:37.920
realism.

01:38.160 --> 01:38.340
Right.

01:38.740 --> 01:43.780
And you start looking at it as a record of problem solving. A whole new landscape of human

01:43.780 --> 01:49.040
cognition opens up. I mean, a drawing basically faces in two distinct directions.

01:49.040 --> 01:50.320
Okay. Two directions.

01:50.480 --> 01:56.140
Yeah. It faces outward toward what it represents, like a person, a dog, a house. But it also

01:56.140 --> 02:01.880
faces backward toward the invisible procedure, the actual sequence of decisions that produced

02:01.880 --> 02:02.580
it on the page.

02:02.800 --> 02:08.380
Okay. Let's unpack this. Because today we are immersing ourselves in a fascinating independent

02:08.380 --> 02:13.280
research essay from September 2026. It's titled, Making Thought Visible.

02:13.520 --> 02:15.560
And it is a brilliant piece of synthesis.

02:15.560 --> 02:20.580
It totally is. It does something incredibly ambitious. It takes two brilliant researchers

02:20.580 --> 02:25.460
separated by like half a century. Jacqueline Goodnow, who did her groundbreaking work way

02:25.460 --> 02:30.000
back in the 1970s, and Judith Phan, who is a modern cognitive scientist.

02:30.260 --> 02:34.000
Right. And it puts their theories into a direct conversation with each other.

02:34.260 --> 02:39.220
Exactly. And our mission for you today, for everyone listening, is to completely change how

02:39.220 --> 02:43.780
you view doodles, sketches, diagrams, and even the data visualizations you use at work

02:43.780 --> 02:47.560
every single day. You'll never look at a whiteboard the same way again.

02:47.840 --> 02:51.720
Seriously. By the time we're done, you are going to see that a drawing isn't just a picture.

02:51.720 --> 02:55.460
It is a literal window into the invisible machinery of human thought.

02:56.400 --> 03:01.720
To truly appreciate how revolutionary the ideas of Goodnow and Phan really are, we first have to

03:01.720 --> 03:06.940
understand the deeply entrenched, honestly outdated ideas they were fighting against in psychology.

03:07.320 --> 03:08.260
The old playbook.

03:08.260 --> 03:14.500
Exactly. The old playbook. If we go back through the 20th century, the way psychologists analyzed

03:14.500 --> 03:20.280
children's drawings was incredibly rigid. The finished image was treated as the ultimate object

03:20.280 --> 03:20.920
of analysis.

03:21.260 --> 03:22.540
Like it was a final exam.

03:22.820 --> 03:29.060
Right. And it was judged primarily through three static lenses. The first of these was the lens of

03:29.060 --> 03:32.340
resemblance, championed by figures like George Henry Luque.

03:32.680 --> 03:34.960
Okay, Luque. What was his deal?

03:34.960 --> 03:40.620
Well, this was basically the idea that cognitive development in drawing was simply a straight,

03:41.000 --> 03:43.000
linear march toward visual realism.

03:43.300 --> 03:47.800
So under Luque's framework, if a kid starts out drawing a house and like makes the walls

03:47.800 --> 03:49.760
transparent so you can see the furniture inside.

03:50.000 --> 03:51.460
The classic x-ray drawing?

03:51.600 --> 03:57.120
Yeah. Or if they draw a table folded out flat so you can see all four legs at once. Under this lens,

03:57.300 --> 03:59.720
they are just in an immature phase.

03:59.720 --> 04:04.500
That's right. They'd say the child is drawing what they know is there rather than what they

04:04.500 --> 04:07.800
literally see from a single realistic vantage point.

04:08.280 --> 04:12.100
And eventually, according to this theory, they will just naturally graduate to true

04:12.100 --> 04:13.200
photographic realism.

04:13.560 --> 04:19.500
That was the prevailing assumption for decades. Any deviation from photographic realism was

04:19.500 --> 04:23.600
considered a failure, like a failure of perception or just bad motor control.

04:23.800 --> 04:25.160
Which feels so dismissive.

04:25.160 --> 04:31.640
It was. Now, the second lens was the diagnostic lens. And the most famous example of this was

04:31.640 --> 04:34.080
Florence Goodenough's Draw a Man test.

04:34.400 --> 04:35.380
Oh, I've heard of this.

04:35.660 --> 04:41.020
Yeah. It was developed in the 1920s. This test literally scored the presence of specific body

04:41.020 --> 04:45.740
parts and details in a child's drawing as a direct index of their intellectual maturity.

04:46.140 --> 04:51.080
Wait, wait. So they were essentially using a stick figure as an actual IQ test?

04:51.380 --> 04:52.280
Literally, yes.

04:52.280 --> 04:55.960
How did that even work mechanically? Did they just count the fingers?

04:56.660 --> 05:01.780
It was essentially a strict checklist. I mean, if the child drew eyebrows, they received a point.

05:02.100 --> 05:06.520
If the arms pointed down instead of, you know, sticking straight out rigidly,

05:06.680 --> 05:08.020
they received another point.

05:08.180 --> 05:08.520
Wow.

05:08.900 --> 05:12.180
If the fingers were distinct and somewhat proportionate, more points.

05:12.940 --> 05:19.520
The drawing was reduced to a mere symptom of a capacity located somewhere else, like general intelligence.

05:19.520 --> 05:21.600
So they didn't care at all about the process?

05:21.600 --> 05:26.620
Not even a little bit. The assessor would tally up the features without any regard for how the

05:26.620 --> 05:31.320
child actually went about constructing the image on the page or what spatial constraints

05:31.320 --> 05:32.620
they might have been fighting against.

05:32.840 --> 05:38.620
That seems incredibly reductive. You're taking this fluid, really creative act and turning it

05:38.620 --> 05:41.140
into a standardized test bubble sheet?

05:41.340 --> 05:42.440
It was highly standardized.

05:42.980 --> 05:47.980
But I mean, it's the third lens that really seems the most subjective to me, the projective

05:47.980 --> 05:48.360
lens.

05:48.500 --> 05:53.920
Ah, yes. The projective lens is perhaps the most culturally pervasive one. It's heavily

05:53.920 --> 05:58.140
associated with researchers like Karen MacCover in the mid-20th century.

05:58.480 --> 05:59.040
Right.

05:59.200 --> 06:04.900
This approach looked at the size, the placement, and the emphasis of different parts of a figure.

06:04.900 --> 06:11.340
And it read them as direct expressions of deep-seated personality traits and unconscious emotional

06:11.340 --> 06:11.960
conflicts.

06:12.480 --> 06:15.500
Which brings us right back to those giant paddle hands I mentioned earlier.

06:16.500 --> 06:21.420
Because under the projective lens, an adult psychologist looks at a child who drew massive,

06:21.720 --> 06:26.620
oversized hands and diagnoses them with some deep, unresolved psychological conflict.

06:27.060 --> 06:29.520
They might say the child is feeling aggressive.

06:29.520 --> 06:35.100
Right. Or maybe they have a fixation on grasping for control in their chaotic family environment

06:35.100 --> 06:36.180
or something like that.

06:36.580 --> 06:38.260
It gets very Freudian very fast.

06:38.520 --> 06:44.040
Totally. But like, diagnosing a kid's emotional state based on the size of a drawn hand feels

06:44.040 --> 06:50.260
completely absurd. It's like looking at someone who packed a remarkably messy suitcase with shirts

06:50.260 --> 06:51.560
bursting out of the zipper.

06:51.940 --> 06:52.140
Right.

06:52.240 --> 06:56.200
And diagnosing them with a psychological fear of folding clothes. You're completely ignoring

06:56.200 --> 07:00.180
the physical reality that their suitcase was simply too small for what they needed to pack.

07:00.960 --> 07:05.660
That analogy cuts right to the heart of Jacqueline Goodenow's critique. I mean, she argued that a far

07:05.660 --> 07:11.340
simpler and much likelier explanation for those oversized hands is just the physical and spatial

07:11.340 --> 07:12.280
difficulty of drawing.

07:12.720 --> 07:13.940
The constraints of the paper.

07:14.360 --> 07:19.740
Exactly. It's about the difficulty of anticipating the problems that each new line on a page creates.

07:19.740 --> 07:26.580
Both Goodenow and Phan completely reject the idea that visual realism is the ultimate natural goal

07:26.580 --> 07:27.140
of a drawing.

07:27.720 --> 07:30.200
Which is such a relief to hear, honestly.

07:30.720 --> 07:36.760
Goodenow, building on the work of early researchers like Rhoda Kellogg, pointed out a fatal flaw in all

07:36.760 --> 07:41.920
three of those old lenses. She observed that children use vastly different techniques within the same

07:41.920 --> 07:44.120
week, sometimes even on the same day.

07:44.560 --> 07:45.900
Really? Like the same kid?

07:45.900 --> 07:52.020
The exact same kid. A child might draw a highly detailed, complex person on Monday, and then a simple

07:52.020 --> 07:53.580
abstract stick figure on Tuesday.

07:54.280 --> 07:59.740
Well, if that's true, then using a single drawing to determine an IQ score or diagnose a personality

07:59.740 --> 08:02.940
disorder is not just misleading, it's actually irresponsible.

08:03.220 --> 08:04.080
Extremely irresponsible.

08:04.380 --> 08:09.680
You're taking a single snapshot of a highly variable process and pretending it represents the totality of the

08:09.680 --> 08:10.260
child's mind.

08:10.480 --> 08:15.860
This raises an important question. If visual realism and anatomical accuracy

08:15.860 --> 08:20.620
are not the actual goals of the drawer, what are the rules they are following?

08:21.320 --> 08:23.080
Yeah, what is actually happening in their heads?

08:23.440 --> 08:29.520
What is the internal logic driving these highly specific, repeating patterns we see in children's art all over the

08:29.520 --> 08:34.660
world? To answer that, we have to tear up the old playbook and step into Jacqueline Goodenow's

08:34.660 --> 08:35.600
conceptual world.

08:36.020 --> 08:42.460
Okay, so we are traveling back to 1977. This is Goodenow's seminal book, Children's Drawing, and this is where we

08:42.460 --> 08:45.420
really begin to examine the backward direction of a drawing.

08:45.680 --> 08:47.400
Right, the backward-facing aspect.

08:47.920 --> 08:53.740
Goodenow essentially tells the field of psychology to stop looking at the drawing as a static noun and start

08:53.740 --> 08:59.660
looking at it as an active verb. She viewed drawing as a sequence of steps where every single step calls for

08:59.660 --> 09:02.300
a choice among alternative next steps.

09:02.300 --> 09:07.980
She treated drawing not as a failed attempt to mirror the external world, but as intense,

09:08.460 --> 09:13.100
logical work. It's work with lines and shapes on a flat, two-dimensional surface.

09:13.340 --> 09:16.360
Which is such a respectful way to view a child's effort.

09:16.540 --> 09:22.040
It really is. She actually grouped the drawing of a human figure together with the act of copying

09:22.040 --> 09:24.000
geometric shapes and drawing maps.

09:24.560 --> 09:26.200
Wow, drawing maps?

09:26.320 --> 09:26.540
Mm-hmm.

09:26.880 --> 09:28.380
That changes the framing entirely.

09:28.380 --> 09:34.140
By making that categorization, she stripped away the obsession with what the drawing depicts

09:34.140 --> 09:40.400
and forced us to focus entirely on the constructional problem the drawer is actively trying to solve.

09:40.860 --> 09:44.000
She was basically hunting for a grammar of action.

09:44.620 --> 09:46.660
A syntax of scribbles, almost.

09:47.020 --> 09:50.040
Oh, I love that phrasing. A syntax of scribbles.

09:50.500 --> 09:55.620
And one of the most fascinating parts of this graphic syntax is the principle of economy.

09:55.620 --> 09:59.360
Because kids are incredibly, almost ruthlessly efficient.

09:59.580 --> 10:00.080
Very true.

10:00.320 --> 10:05.540
Goodnow points out that children often reuse the exact same graphic unit for multiple different

10:05.540 --> 10:10.620
purposes within a single drawing. The classic example she pulls from Rudolf Arnheim is the heart

10:10.620 --> 10:10.980
shape.

10:11.560 --> 10:13.340
The heart shape is a perfect example.

10:13.740 --> 10:15.920
Imagine a standard symmetrical heart.

10:16.360 --> 10:21.500
A child might draw a large heart shape and use it for the torso. But then they might draw two

10:21.500 --> 10:23.820
smaller heart shapes and use them for the eyes.

10:24.320 --> 10:28.940
Right. And then a medium one becomes the mouth. They might even use heart shapes for the hands

10:28.940 --> 10:29.900
or the ears.

10:30.540 --> 10:35.760
Now, under the old Leuke lens of resemblance, an adult would look at that repetition and conclude

10:35.760 --> 10:39.060
the child has a severely impoverished visual vocabulary.

10:39.580 --> 10:42.180
They'd be like, this kid literally doesn't know what ears look like.

10:42.300 --> 10:42.660
Exactly.

10:42.660 --> 10:48.000
They would assume the child doesn't know what hands look like. But Arnheim and Goodnow argued

10:48.000 --> 10:52.680
the exact opposite. This economy is a massive achievement of visual thought.

10:52.920 --> 10:53.780
It's clever.

10:54.180 --> 10:59.700
It's the discovery of abstract similarity. The child is essentially saying, I have mastered

10:59.700 --> 11:05.420
the motor sequence required to produce this specific closed form. Now, how many spatial

11:05.420 --> 11:07.880
problems can I solve with this one tool?

11:07.880 --> 11:11.060
So, the form comes first and the meaning is assigned later.

11:11.220 --> 11:12.420
Yes. Precisely.

11:12.760 --> 11:17.200
The source text brings up Rhoda Kellogg's work here, specifically regarding shapes like mandalas.

11:17.320 --> 11:21.260
You know, those closed, usually oval forms with crossed lines inside.

11:21.440 --> 11:22.940
Right. And radials, too.

11:23.400 --> 11:29.300
Yeah. Radials, which look like little sunbursts or asterisks. Kids practice these shapes endlessly,

11:29.780 --> 11:34.000
not because they are trying to draw the sun or a pie, but simply because the shapes feel

11:34.000 --> 11:35.380
physically good to draw.

11:35.380 --> 11:38.520
They possess a highly satisfying visual balance.

11:39.060 --> 11:43.660
Exactly. And only later do they slap a radial on top of a body and declare it to be hair,

11:43.860 --> 11:48.560
or stick it on the end of a stick arm and call it a hand. They aren't trying to draw a hand.

11:48.880 --> 11:52.320
They are trying to find a semantic job for their favorite radial shape.

11:52.760 --> 11:58.280
That's brilliant. And we can see this logic extending into the strict rules that govern how

11:58.280 --> 12:03.560
these shapes are allowed to interact on the page. Goodnow observed that young children operate

12:03.560 --> 12:06.740
with two incredibly rigid, self-imposed principles.

12:07.240 --> 12:08.100
Okay. What are they?

12:08.640 --> 12:11.780
To each its own boundary and to each its own space.

12:12.240 --> 12:13.220
Okay. Explain that.

12:13.640 --> 12:19.860
Well, young children abhor overlapping lines. They tend to meticulously fence parts off from

12:19.860 --> 12:25.460
one another. They prefer to leave a kind of empty no-man's land between the head and the arms

12:25.460 --> 12:27.000
or the torso and the legs.

12:27.720 --> 12:29.080
I can picture that.

12:29.480 --> 12:34.780
They will draw a circle for the head, stop, lift the pen, move it down through empty space,

12:34.880 --> 12:36.180
and then begin the torso.

12:36.540 --> 12:41.100
But eventually, as they get older, they grow out of that isolationist phase, right?

12:41.260 --> 12:44.800
They start wanting to draw what Goodnow calls an embracing line.

12:44.960 --> 12:46.280
Yes, the embracing line.

12:46.280 --> 12:51.060
Which is like a single, continuous stroke of the pen that forms the head and the body

12:51.060 --> 12:53.980
or the body and the arms all at once without lifting the marker.

12:54.480 --> 12:56.120
It's a huge developmental milestone.

12:56.840 --> 12:59.180
The paper notes this is a huge intellectual shift.

12:59.600 --> 13:02.780
The kid isn't just listing parts like a visual grocery list anymore.

13:03.240 --> 13:07.420
They are mentally coordinating complex anatomical relationships in real time.

13:08.000 --> 13:12.580
But, as Goodnow so brilliantly puts it, a problem solved is a problem created.

13:13.200 --> 13:15.280
That is the eternal truth of drawing.

13:15.280 --> 13:18.300
When a child attempts an embracing line for the first time,

13:18.800 --> 13:22.280
they run into a massive, cascading spatial management issue.

13:22.840 --> 13:23.460
A traffic jam.

13:23.820 --> 13:24.980
A huge traffic jam.

13:25.580 --> 13:29.840
Imagine a child trying to draw an arm and a hand with one continuous line.

13:30.480 --> 13:34.220
They trace down the side of the body, extend the line out for the top of the arm,

13:34.820 --> 13:39.100
curve around for the hand, and then bring the line back under the arm to connect to the torso.

13:39.580 --> 13:40.700
Sounds fine so far.

13:41.040 --> 13:44.400
The problem arises when they realize they must incorporate the fingers

13:44.400 --> 13:46.960
into that single, unbroken journey of the pen.

13:47.660 --> 13:48.180
Oh, no.

13:48.820 --> 13:51.400
And here is the true origin of the banana hands.

13:51.860 --> 13:52.720
You've figured it out.

13:53.080 --> 13:56.240
If the child is stubbornly adhering to their new rule, like,

13:56.460 --> 13:58.300
do not lift the pen, use an embracing line,

13:58.420 --> 14:02.720
and they suddenly realize they have to squeeze five distinct, looping fingers

14:02.720 --> 14:04.920
into the narrow width of the arm they just drew...

14:04.920 --> 14:07.220
The line has nowhere to go but out.

14:07.700 --> 14:11.440
It has to bulge out massively to accommodate all those directional changes.

14:11.880 --> 14:13.640
The arm becomes a thick tree trunk.

14:13.840 --> 14:15.820
The hand balloons into a giant boxing glove.

14:16.080 --> 14:16.480
Exactly.

14:16.480 --> 14:21.460
It has absolutely nothing to do with deep psychological trauma about grasping at the world,

14:21.600 --> 14:22.740
as Mack Over might have claimed.

14:23.060 --> 14:25.540
It's literally just a geometric traffic jam.

14:25.800 --> 14:27.160
It's physics, not Freud.

14:27.160 --> 14:31.340
And Goodnow didn't just observe these traffic jams in free drawings.

14:31.720 --> 14:34.420
She actively manufactured them to test her theories.

14:34.580 --> 14:35.660
Yes, the experiments.

14:36.260 --> 14:39.260
This is where her work transitions from observational theory

14:39.260 --> 14:41.580
into rigorous experimental psychology.

14:41.940 --> 14:44.500
She wanted to prove that these internal boundary rules

14:44.500 --> 14:46.840
were genuinely dictating the children's choices

14:46.840 --> 14:50.040
rather than just being a post-hoc description of their art.

14:50.180 --> 14:51.460
So she designed tests.

14:51.660 --> 14:51.840
Right.

14:51.840 --> 14:57.240
To do this, she utilized what cognitive scientists call inherited figure interventions.

14:57.900 --> 14:59.920
She would present children with a piece of paper

14:59.920 --> 15:03.140
where a portion of the drawing was already completed by an adult.

15:03.300 --> 15:05.900
Okay, so the kids inherit a partially finished drawing.

15:06.120 --> 15:06.600
Precisely.

15:06.920 --> 15:11.300
But these pre-drawn figures were carefully designed to force a conflict.

15:11.940 --> 15:16.720
A conflict between the child's internal spatial rules and the demands of reality.

15:17.080 --> 15:20.080
These experiments are easily my favorite part of the source material.

15:20.080 --> 15:22.960
Let's break down the hair versus arms experiment

15:22.960 --> 15:25.140
because it is so funny and so revealing.

15:25.360 --> 15:26.560
It really is.

15:27.020 --> 15:29.700
Goodnow had noticed that in spontaneous free play drawings,

15:29.840 --> 15:32.200
young kids almost never draw both hair and arms

15:32.200 --> 15:33.900
originating from a single head circle.

15:34.220 --> 15:35.680
It's usually one or the other.

15:35.820 --> 15:36.100
Right.

15:36.520 --> 15:38.960
If you ask why, the answer is purely spatial.

15:39.400 --> 15:43.400
Arms, hair, and ears are all competing for the exact same premium real estate

15:43.400 --> 15:46.000
around the limited perimeter of that one circle.

15:46.280 --> 15:48.340
It's very crowded around the tadpole head.

15:48.640 --> 15:49.120
Exactly.

15:49.120 --> 15:53.180
So to test the absolute strength of this no overlapping rule,

15:53.520 --> 15:55.960
she gave preschoolers a piece of paper with a large circle

15:55.960 --> 15:58.600
and two vertical sticks already drawn descending from it.

15:58.900 --> 16:00.960
Basically, a very basic head and legs.

16:01.160 --> 16:02.320
A standard starter kit.

16:02.520 --> 16:02.700
Right.

16:03.300 --> 16:07.740
She then explicitly instructed the children to add both arms and hair to the figure.

16:08.120 --> 16:12.980
Now remember, overlapping lines is absolute anathema to their internal syntax.

16:13.200 --> 16:14.860
It's the one rule they don't want to break.

16:14.860 --> 16:20.260
So the kids immediately start performing these crazy cognitive acrobatics to avoid crossing

16:20.260 --> 16:20.740
those boundaries.

16:21.400 --> 16:25.260
If Goodnow handed them a figure that already had arms sticking straight out of the sides

16:25.260 --> 16:27.920
of the head and said, please draw hair.

16:28.420 --> 16:29.400
The kids panic.

16:29.640 --> 16:29.800
Yeah.

16:30.020 --> 16:34.520
They would draw the hair at crazy, steep vertical angles right at the very top of the circle,

16:35.020 --> 16:38.600
clustering it tightly just to avoid letting the hair even touch the arms.

16:38.600 --> 16:40.500
Like a severe mohawk.

16:40.840 --> 16:41.080
Yes.

16:41.880 --> 16:45.500
Conversely, if she gave them a figure that already had hair sprawling down the sides of

16:45.500 --> 16:50.520
the face and said, please draw arms, they would drop the arms way down the body or angle

16:50.520 --> 16:51.620
them sharply toward the floor.

16:52.000 --> 16:54.080
They'll do anything to keep the spaces separate.

16:54.720 --> 16:55.080
Anything.

16:55.080 --> 16:58.360
But the most revealing response wasn't a drawing at all.

16:58.920 --> 17:03.040
Some children would look at the paper, see that the arms were already taking up the lateral

17:03.040 --> 17:06.840
space, and simply declare the request completely impossible.

17:07.360 --> 17:08.720
They literally refuse.

17:09.180 --> 17:13.320
They would look up at the researcher and politely offer to draw a hat instead.

17:13.860 --> 17:18.620
A child offering to draw a hat instead of hair is a profound psychological moment.

17:18.940 --> 17:21.520
It's so funny, but also so deep.

17:21.520 --> 17:25.220
That child has not forgotten that human beings possess hair.

17:25.660 --> 17:26.660
It's not a memory issue.

17:27.140 --> 17:31.520
They are actively protecting their internal spatial syntax at all costs.

17:31.740 --> 17:31.840
Right.

17:32.320 --> 17:38.060
The procedural rule of do not cross boundaries is ranked significantly higher in their cognitive

17:38.060 --> 17:42.280
hierarchy than the semantic rule of accurately depict human anatomy.

17:42.720 --> 17:44.200
They just override biology.

17:44.560 --> 17:49.720
As Goodnow concluded from this, to meet one goal, we will routinely sacrifice another.

17:49.720 --> 17:55.780
The deviation from physical reality is actually the elegant solution to a spatial problem,

17:56.200 --> 17:57.560
not a perceptual error.

17:58.200 --> 18:02.280
They would literally rather break the laws of biology than cross a line on a page.

18:02.560 --> 18:05.880
And she proved this hierarchical ranking again with the train wheels experiment, which

18:05.880 --> 18:07.100
takes this logic even further.

18:07.660 --> 18:09.860
The train experiment is brilliant.

18:10.520 --> 18:12.320
It built upon previous work by N.H.

18:12.400 --> 18:12.660
Freeman.

18:12.760 --> 18:13.060
Oh, right.

18:13.140 --> 18:14.520
The aerial perspective stuff.

18:14.800 --> 18:15.220
Exactly.

18:15.920 --> 18:19.700
Freeman had demonstrated that children possess a very strong preference for placing a

18:19.700 --> 18:22.120
appendages in free, unoccupied space.

18:22.600 --> 18:26.540
If a child draws an animal, they will frequently employ an aerial perspective.

18:27.020 --> 18:28.680
Meaning they draw legs pointing everywhere?

18:29.060 --> 18:29.400
Yes.

18:29.660 --> 18:33.820
They might draw two legs pointing down below the body and two legs pointing straight up,

18:34.000 --> 18:35.740
floating above the body into the sky.

18:36.100 --> 18:37.440
So weird looking.

18:37.800 --> 18:39.340
But it solves the spacing issue.

18:39.340 --> 18:45.120
However, if you force their hand by putting a pre-drawn rider on the animal's back, thereby

18:45.120 --> 18:47.180
occupying that upper free space...

18:47.180 --> 18:48.440
The child instantly adapts.

18:48.600 --> 18:48.820
Right.

18:49.120 --> 18:51.840
They put all four legs below the animal where they belong.

18:52.800 --> 18:56.840
Goodenow and her colleague Rosalind Dawes took this established logic and applied it to

18:56.840 --> 18:57.940
a mechanical object.

18:58.520 --> 18:59.000
A train.

18:59.460 --> 18:59.660
Okay.

18:59.780 --> 19:00.960
So paint the picture for us.

19:01.040 --> 19:02.580
What did they actually give the kids?

19:02.580 --> 19:08.640
They presented kids, ranging from three to seven years old, with a line drawing of a simple

19:08.640 --> 19:09.300
train carriage.

19:09.940 --> 19:13.840
This carriage already had two large wheels drawn on the bottom, and those wheels were

19:13.840 --> 19:16.580
touching a solid, horizontal ground line.

19:17.120 --> 19:19.340
So the bottom is totally blocked off.

19:19.940 --> 19:20.980
Completely occupied.

19:21.760 --> 19:23.800
The researchers then told the kids a little story.

19:24.440 --> 19:29.360
The person who drew this train wanted to add two more wheels, but they didn't know how.

19:29.360 --> 19:30.920
Can you help them do it?

19:31.640 --> 19:36.160
Now, if you are a child trying to do this realistically, you only have a few options, and they all involve

19:36.160 --> 19:37.220
breaking a rule, right?

19:37.800 --> 19:38.160
Right.

19:38.640 --> 19:43.700
You either have to draw the new wheels overlapping the old wheels, which breaks the boundary rule,

19:43.880 --> 19:47.600
or you have to draw them overlapping the bottom line of the carriage itself.

19:48.360 --> 19:49.800
Which also breaks the rule.

19:50.440 --> 19:50.840
Exactly.

19:51.220 --> 19:56.940
Both of those realistic solutions require violating the syntax, and out of nearly a hundred children

19:56.940 --> 20:00.640
tested, only a single child chose to overlap the wheels.

20:01.360 --> 20:03.060
One kid out of a hundred?

20:03.840 --> 20:04.440
Just one.

20:04.820 --> 20:09.320
The rest of the cohort generated a fascinating array of alternatives to preserve their syntax.

20:09.800 --> 20:13.580
Some drew wheels floating completely detached above the carriage in the sky.

20:14.060 --> 20:14.980
Like hover trains.

20:15.560 --> 20:15.800
Yeah.

20:16.320 --> 20:21.340
Some drew tiny, mismatched wheels squeezed awkwardly into the extreme corners of the carriage box.

20:21.740 --> 20:24.820
Some drew the extra wheels floating off to the left or right side.

20:24.820 --> 20:26.220
They get so creative.

20:26.920 --> 20:30.600
But the most sophisticated, high-effort solution was a structural one.

20:31.340 --> 20:35.940
Several children literally drew an entirely new, empty train carriage extending off the

20:35.940 --> 20:36.760
back of the first one.

20:37.020 --> 20:38.280
That is just wild.

20:38.860 --> 20:44.600
They expended all that extra effort just so they would have a fresh, unoccupied, mathematically

20:44.600 --> 20:47.500
clean underside to attach the two new wheels to.

20:47.500 --> 20:50.580
I can't get over the logic of that.

20:50.660 --> 20:55.540
It's like the child is saying, um, I need to place more wheels, but this universe is currently

20:55.540 --> 20:55.860
full.

20:56.080 --> 20:56.300
Fine.

20:56.380 --> 20:58.320
I'll just build an expansion pack to the universe.

20:58.720 --> 21:00.200
It's a genius workaround.

21:00.540 --> 21:05.440
They literally redefined the initial constraints of the problem to wipe out the difficulty entirely.

21:05.660 --> 21:11.380
It's a completely different kind of intelligence than what that old draw-a-man IQ test was

21:11.380 --> 21:11.900
looking for.

21:11.900 --> 21:16.440
It is a procedural intelligence deeply concerned with the physics of the page.

21:17.600 --> 21:22.200
Goodnow's lasting legacy is showing us that a finished drawing is a delicate resolution

21:22.200 --> 21:24.480
among competing internal demands.

21:25.240 --> 21:26.180
The backward direction.

21:26.600 --> 21:26.980
Exactly.

21:27.540 --> 21:32.960
But if Goodnow was the pioneer of understanding the backward direction, the internal rules,

21:33.140 --> 21:38.180
the sequence, the spatial constraints of the drawer, our modern researcher Judith Phan

21:38.180 --> 21:40.540
is the master of the outward direction.

21:40.540 --> 21:44.340
Right, because Phan approaches drawing from a completely different angle.

21:44.780 --> 21:48.080
She's asking, who is this drawing actually for?

21:48.680 --> 21:54.460
If Goodnow shows us the drawer's private, internal rulebook, Phan's modern cognitive science program

21:54.460 --> 21:55.540
shows us the audience.

21:56.660 --> 22:01.720
Phan views drawing not simply as a sequence of mechanical marks, but as a highly versatile

22:01.720 --> 22:04.900
cognitive tool used primarily for social communication.

22:05.220 --> 22:06.880
It's about passing a message.

22:07.120 --> 22:07.500
Exactly.

22:07.500 --> 22:13.600
It's about how we use physical representations to learn, to solve problems, and most importantly,

22:13.800 --> 22:16.260
to transmit complex ideas into other minds.

22:17.060 --> 22:21.540
So for Phan, a drawing is fundamentally an act of massive data compression.

22:21.800 --> 22:22.760
Data compression, yes.

22:23.100 --> 22:28.340
Consider the act of drawing a bustling city street or, I don't know, a complex mechanical gear

22:28.340 --> 22:28.680
system.

22:28.680 --> 22:35.540
You are taking an incredibly rich, multidimensional referent in the real world, and you are aggressively

22:35.540 --> 22:38.680
reducing it to a few simple two-dimensional marks on a page.

22:38.900 --> 22:40.820
You're throwing away a lot of information.

22:41.140 --> 22:41.600
Tons of it.

22:41.900 --> 22:44.760
The fundamental question driving Phan's research is this.

22:45.360 --> 22:50.100
What decides which properties survive this extreme reduction and which are thrown away?

22:50.360 --> 22:53.060
And her answer is contextual flexibility.

22:53.460 --> 22:54.540
Contextual flexibility.

22:54.540 --> 23:00.160
The features that survive are selected based entirely on what the drawing is for and who

23:00.160 --> 23:01.320
is going to be looking at it.

23:01.860 --> 23:03.540
Here's where it gets really interesting.

23:04.400 --> 23:08.000
Think about how your personal interaction history with someone changes how you communicate

23:08.000 --> 23:08.380
with them.

23:09.080 --> 23:13.420
Phan's group conducted studies looking at how people alter their drawings based on the intended

23:13.420 --> 23:13.960
audience.

23:14.180 --> 23:14.400
Right.

23:14.560 --> 23:16.460
The stranger versus friend dynamic.

23:16.460 --> 23:16.820
Yeah.

23:17.780 --> 23:20.560
Imagine you are asked to draw a dog for a complete stranger.

23:20.960 --> 23:25.460
You are going to put in a significant amount of effort to include what they call category

23:25.460 --> 23:26.560
diagnostic features.

23:27.080 --> 23:31.960
You'll carefully draw the snout, the floppy ears, four distinct legs, and a tail, striving

23:31.960 --> 23:35.280
to make it as universally recognizable as a dog as possible.

23:35.680 --> 23:37.440
Because the stranger has no context.

23:37.600 --> 23:37.960
Exactly.

23:37.960 --> 23:42.540
But what if you were drawing that exact same dog for your best friend, who happens to have

23:42.540 --> 23:46.400
been standing right next to you an hour ago when that specific dog did something hilarious

23:46.400 --> 23:47.080
with its tail?

23:47.500 --> 23:48.500
That changes everything.

23:48.820 --> 23:53.200
For your friend, you might just draw a single jagged squiggly line representing the tail,

23:53.460 --> 23:56.340
and they will look at it and know exactly what you mean.

23:56.660 --> 24:01.100
The shared interaction history essentially carries the bulk of the semantic meaning.

24:01.800 --> 24:07.080
The physical drawing can afford to be incredibly sparse because the cognitive burden of interpretation

24:07.080 --> 24:10.200
is distributed between the drawer and the viewer.

24:11.120 --> 24:12.480
It's an inside joke, basically.

24:12.800 --> 24:13.240
It is.

24:13.640 --> 24:19.340
This powerfully demonstrates that there is no single best or most realistic drawing of

24:19.340 --> 24:20.820
an object out there in the ether.

24:21.220 --> 24:25.720
There's only an optimal drawing for a specific goal and a specific audience.

24:26.080 --> 24:31.600
And Fan's team tested this dynamic explicitly with a study called the Depiction vs. Explanation

24:31.600 --> 24:33.960
Experiment, led by researcher Holly Huey.

24:34.760 --> 24:36.140
A very clever study design.

24:36.140 --> 24:40.520
They brought people into a lab and asked them to draw a novel, highly unfamiliar mechanical

24:40.520 --> 24:40.980
object.

24:41.400 --> 24:44.360
It was something with gears, pulleys, and structural supports.

24:44.800 --> 24:48.880
But they split the participants into two groups with two vastly different goals.

24:49.320 --> 24:52.220
The depiction group and the explanation group.

24:52.580 --> 24:52.860
Right.

24:53.380 --> 24:57.400
Group A was instructed to draw the object so that a viewer could accurately pick it out of

24:57.400 --> 24:58.420
a visual lineup later.

24:58.660 --> 25:00.900
Their goal was purely to depict it visually.

25:01.540 --> 25:06.000
Group B was instructed to draw the object so that a viewer could understand how the mechanism

25:06.000 --> 25:07.100
actually works.

25:07.600 --> 25:08.940
Their goal was to explain it.

25:09.260 --> 25:14.860
The divergence in the resulting drawings perfectly illustrates Fan's concept of goal-sensitive

25:14.860 --> 25:15.400
compression.

25:16.060 --> 25:18.240
So what did the drawings actually look like?

25:18.740 --> 25:24.240
Well, the drawings from the depiction group carefully preserved superficial visual features.

25:24.240 --> 25:29.920
They focused on the exact outer shape, the shading, the texture, and the background details.

25:30.080 --> 25:34.040
Basically all the things that are highly useful for visual identification and matching.

25:34.300 --> 25:34.840
Exactly.

25:35.200 --> 25:40.760
But the drawings from the explanation group actively suppressed and discarded those same superficial

25:40.760 --> 25:41.240
details.

25:41.240 --> 25:47.980
Instead, they prioritized the unseen causal parts, the functional relationships between the gears

25:47.980 --> 25:49.120
and the potential for motion.

25:49.560 --> 25:51.140
They probably used arrows, right?

25:51.540 --> 25:55.140
Lots of abstract symbols like arrows to denote rotation.

25:55.500 --> 25:58.400
They stripped the mechanism down to its conceptual skeleton.

25:59.180 --> 26:04.040
And predictably, when a separate group of viewers looked at these drawings, they extracted

26:04.040 --> 26:08.260
mechanistic, functional information much more readily from the explanatory drawings.

26:08.260 --> 26:12.180
And they identified the object's external appearance much better from the depictions.

26:12.480 --> 26:12.920
Exactly.

26:13.420 --> 26:16.500
Which completely shatters the old Leuke lens of realism.

26:16.780 --> 26:17.560
It totally does.

26:18.000 --> 26:23.260
If you just judge a drawing by how quote-unquote realistic or photographically recognizable it

26:23.260 --> 26:26.820
is, you might completely miss its actual brilliance and utility.

26:27.600 --> 26:32.700
A highly realistic, beautifully shaded drawing of a gear system might be an absolutely terrible

26:32.700 --> 26:36.580
tool for teaching an apprentice how that gear system actually transfers power.

26:36.860 --> 26:37.940
It would be too cluttered.

26:37.940 --> 26:38.260
Yeah.

26:38.920 --> 26:42.880
But Fan didn't just limit this research to adult sketching in controlled university labs.

26:43.320 --> 26:47.800
Her team looked at children's cognitive development at a truly massive scale.

26:48.020 --> 26:50.380
The scale of this particular study is staggering.

26:50.760 --> 26:54.880
It represents one of the most comprehensive data collections in recent cognitive science.

26:54.960 --> 26:56.080
It was the museum one, right?

26:56.220 --> 26:56.400
Right.

26:56.580 --> 27:02.320
Researcher Brea Long and her colleagues collected over 37,000 drawings from children ranging in

27:02.320 --> 27:07.040
age from 2 to 10 years old, utilizing a digital drawing station set up at a public children's

27:07.040 --> 27:07.400
museum.

27:07.400 --> 27:09.860
Over 37,000 drawings.

27:09.960 --> 27:10.040
Right.

27:10.040 --> 27:12.100
That is just a mountain of data.

27:12.220 --> 27:12.840
It's massive.

27:13.420 --> 27:16.900
They had these kids draw from 48 different categories of things.

27:17.480 --> 27:20.600
Bees, cats, couches, trains, you name it.

27:20.600 --> 27:21.480
And what did they find?

27:21.480 --> 27:24.320
I assume the older kids drew better.

27:24.700 --> 27:25.440
Logically, yes.

27:26.080 --> 27:31.160
As kids get older, their drawings begin to carry more category diagnostic information.

27:31.740 --> 27:37.360
Meaning, if you feed the drawing to a machine vision model, an AI, it can more easily and

27:37.360 --> 27:42.980
accurately recognize that this specific arrangement of pixels represents a dog or a couch.

27:42.980 --> 27:43.420
Right.

27:43.520 --> 27:44.040
That makes sense.

27:44.440 --> 27:47.660
But the truly mind-blowing part of the study wasn't about the older kids.

27:48.300 --> 27:51.400
It was what they discovered hiding inside the scribbles of the toddlers.

27:51.840 --> 27:52.120
Okay.

27:52.300 --> 27:53.500
Brace yourself for this one.

27:54.080 --> 27:58.240
Even in the drawings produced by the very youngest children, we're talking two and three-year-olds,

27:58.860 --> 28:03.880
drawings that human adults would confidently classify as completely unrecognizable chaotic

28:03.880 --> 28:04.460
scribbles.

28:04.700 --> 28:06.540
Just absolute crayon chaos.

28:06.760 --> 28:07.020
Right.

28:07.380 --> 28:12.240
The machine vision models could still detect underlying structured information in that chaos.

28:12.240 --> 28:17.160
The AI could reliably tell whether the scribble was meant to represent something animate,

28:17.420 --> 28:21.620
like a dog or a bird, or something inanimate, like a couch or a cup.

28:21.920 --> 28:22.300
Wow.

28:22.700 --> 28:27.100
Furthermore, the models could detect embedded information about the real-world physical size

28:27.100 --> 28:31.720
of the object, all hidden within the seemingly frantic loops and lines of a toddler's crayon.

28:32.200 --> 28:35.120
Wait, so the scribbles aren't just motor noise.

28:35.220 --> 28:36.840
They are actually encoded data?

28:37.080 --> 28:38.320
They are encoded data.

28:38.660 --> 28:39.460
That is wild.

28:39.460 --> 28:45.360
So when a three-year-old aggressively scribbles a tiny, dense, dark knot of ink in the center

28:45.360 --> 28:50.520
of the page, they might be actively encoding the concepts of small and alive, like a bug?

28:50.820 --> 28:51.300
Exactly.

28:51.780 --> 28:57.240
And when they make massive, sweeping, page-wide loops that use up the whole canvas, they might

28:57.240 --> 29:00.140
be encoding big and stationary, like a house?

29:00.340 --> 29:00.680
Yes.

29:00.680 --> 29:05.940
They are preserving abstract information about the world at multiple grains simultaneously,

29:06.240 --> 29:11.080
even if they don't have the fine motor control to draw a window or an antenna yet.

29:11.740 --> 29:17.300
This modern AI analysis beautifully reframes GoodNow's analog findings from 50 years prior.

29:17.800 --> 29:20.340
Remember GoodNow noticing kids substituting shapes?

29:20.340 --> 29:23.420
A heart for an ear, a radial for a hand?

29:23.720 --> 29:25.180
Yes, the principle of economy.

29:25.720 --> 29:30.860
Fan's work suggests that these graphic equivalents aren't just arbitrary, convenient substitutions

29:30.860 --> 29:31.540
for reality.

29:32.100 --> 29:37.560
They are highly selective carriers of information, perfectly tailored to the distinctions a viewer

29:37.560 --> 29:38.100
must make.

29:38.600 --> 29:41.440
The child isn't just learning how to make a physical form.

29:41.560 --> 29:46.600
They are learning how to make a form carry a specific communicative distinction.

29:46.600 --> 29:50.520
Okay, let's step back and look at the whole board for a second.

29:51.060 --> 29:56.280
We have Jacqueline GoodNow showing us the internal, backward-facing rules of spatial construction.

29:56.720 --> 30:02.060
And we have Judith Phan showing us the external, outward-facing rules of social communication

30:02.060 --> 30:03.160
and data compression.

30:03.480 --> 30:05.540
Two totally different directions.

30:06.080 --> 30:09.540
How on earth do we put them together into a unified theory of drawing?

30:09.660 --> 30:09.880
Right.

30:09.940 --> 30:10.780
How do they connect?

30:11.200 --> 30:16.580
The essay proposes that to merge these two views, we have to conceptualize a single

30:16.580 --> 30:22.260
drawing as something that can be decomposed, broken down into its fundamental constituent

30:22.260 --> 30:25.160
parts, in three completely different ways.

30:25.700 --> 30:27.180
Three decompositions.

30:27.300 --> 30:28.420
Okay, walk us through them.

30:28.860 --> 30:32.740
This is a crucial conceptual leap for understanding visual cognition.

30:33.640 --> 30:37.100
Let's isolate a single graphic unit from a child's drawing.

30:37.280 --> 30:39.880
Let's say a simple hand-drawn oval.

30:40.200 --> 30:40.900
Just an oval.

30:41.760 --> 30:42.000
Okay.

30:42.000 --> 30:47.980
The first way to decompose or describe that oval in the context of the drawing is the graphic

30:47.980 --> 30:48.680
decomposition.

30:49.500 --> 30:52.320
This looks purely at the constructional type, the geometry.

30:52.820 --> 30:57.160
It simply notes that the child produced an oval shape of a certain size and trajectory.

30:57.800 --> 30:59.640
Just the physical ink on the page.

31:00.320 --> 31:00.580
Right.

31:01.060 --> 31:04.700
The second way to look at it is the semantic decomposition.

31:05.380 --> 31:09.300
This asks, what does this oval actually represent in the real world?

31:09.300 --> 31:14.560
And as we learned from Goodenow's principle of economy, that exact same graphic oval could

31:14.560 --> 31:21.000
semantically represent an arm, a petal on a flower, a balloon, or a foot, depending entirely

31:21.000 --> 31:21.800
on the context.

31:22.120 --> 31:22.500
Exactly.

31:23.240 --> 31:28.440
And the third way, the layer that bridges the physical and the mental, is the procedural

31:28.440 --> 31:29.280
decomposition.

31:30.460 --> 31:31.020
Procedural.

31:31.680 --> 31:36.660
This looks at the precise order in which the units were produced in time and how they

31:36.660 --> 31:38.140
structurally depend on one another.

31:38.640 --> 31:44.120
Did the oval come first, serving as a foundation, or was it anchored to a previously drawn line?

31:44.800 --> 31:48.660
Did drawing the oval foreclose the possibility of drawing a larger shape later?

31:48.980 --> 31:52.720
So, one drawing, three totally distinct descriptions.

31:53.300 --> 31:58.300
The geometric shapes used, the real world meanings assigned to them, and the chronological order

31:58.300 --> 31:59.140
they were drawn in.

31:59.240 --> 31:59.820
You've got it.

31:59.820 --> 32:04.360
And the essay gives this brilliant, almost detective-like example of how these three layers

32:04.360 --> 32:09.000
interact to solve a mystery, using what Goodenow called the belly button analysis.

32:09.420 --> 32:14.700
Oh, the belly button analysis perfectly illustrates how the procedural timeline and the semantic

32:14.700 --> 32:16.860
meaning are deeply intertwined.

32:17.060 --> 32:17.720
Tell us about it.

32:18.220 --> 32:22.820
Goodenow was analyzing a very common type of children's drawing, the human figure that

32:22.820 --> 32:27.640
consists of just a single circle with two vertical sticks descending directly from the bottom

32:27.640 --> 32:27.960
of it.

32:28.160 --> 32:29.140
The classic tadpole.

32:29.140 --> 32:31.840
The circle and sticks figure we talked about at the top of the show.

32:31.980 --> 32:32.160
Right.

32:32.580 --> 32:36.900
Now, the dilemma for the adult researcher looking at this finished image is this.

32:37.820 --> 32:41.520
What is the actual semantic meaning of that single circle?

32:41.840 --> 32:43.400
I mean, I'd assume it's a head.

32:43.680 --> 32:44.360
It could be.

32:44.760 --> 32:48.580
Is it meant to be just a head with the sticks representing legs growing out of a chin?

32:49.120 --> 32:54.020
Or is the circle meant to be a combined head and trunk unit with the sticks representing

32:54.020 --> 32:54.380
legs?

32:54.760 --> 32:55.700
Oh, I didn't even think of that.

32:55.700 --> 33:01.760
Or, and this is the really tricky one, is the empty white space between the two sticks

33:01.760 --> 33:05.060
actually meant to be the invisible trunk of the body?

33:05.060 --> 33:10.340
If you are just looking at the finished image on the fridge, you have absolutely no idea.

33:10.560 --> 33:12.700
The meaning is fundamentally ambiguous.

33:13.320 --> 33:14.280
It's impossible to know.

33:14.920 --> 33:17.480
So, Goodenow deployed a procedural trick.

33:18.060 --> 33:22.480
She asked the children to take their finished drawing and add just one specific detail.

33:23.140 --> 33:23.980
A belly button.

33:24.400 --> 33:25.260
Just a simple dot.

33:25.560 --> 33:26.160
Just a dot.

33:26.160 --> 33:32.280
And the physical placement of that single new future mark retroactively revealed the

33:32.280 --> 33:34.040
hidden meaning of the old marks.

33:34.600 --> 33:34.920
Precisely.

33:35.120 --> 33:36.560
It was a genius intervention.

33:37.420 --> 33:39.640
And the results were remarkably consistent.

33:40.580 --> 33:44.540
She found that the placement of the belly button depended heavily on the graphic proportions

33:44.540 --> 33:46.400
the child had already established.

33:46.840 --> 33:47.340
How so?

33:47.340 --> 33:53.460
Well, if the child had drawn a circle that was quite long and elongated relative to the

33:53.460 --> 33:58.980
short stick legs beneath it, they almost always placed the new belly button inside the bottom

33:58.980 --> 33:59.760
half of the circle.

34:00.220 --> 34:01.060
Ah, okay.

34:01.460 --> 34:06.680
By doing that, they definitively revealed that the circle was, semantically, functioning as

34:06.680 --> 34:08.400
a combined head and trunk.

34:08.720 --> 34:09.680
That makes total sense.

34:09.940 --> 34:14.680
But if the child had drawn a small, tight circle with really long, sprawling stick legs,

34:15.220 --> 34:15.820
where did it go?

34:15.820 --> 34:19.940
They would draw the belly button floating in the empty white space exactly between the

34:19.940 --> 34:20.580
two sticks.

34:21.220 --> 34:21.780
Wow.

34:22.180 --> 34:28.280
So they were treating the negative space bounded by the legs as the invisible, implied trunk

34:28.280 --> 34:28.760
of the body.

34:28.980 --> 34:29.300
Yes.

34:29.780 --> 34:31.160
But consider the timeline here.

34:31.580 --> 34:35.940
Does this mean the meaning of the initial circle isn't actually locked in the child's

34:35.940 --> 34:38.200
mind until the belly button is drawn?

34:38.500 --> 34:41.000
Does the future mark define the past mark?

34:41.000 --> 34:45.620
It is more accurate to say that the future mark is what allows the viewer to confidently

34:45.620 --> 34:48.120
recover the semantic meaning of the past mark.

34:48.720 --> 34:49.300
Okay, I see.

34:49.700 --> 34:53.860
You don't figure out what the drawing means by asking what it looks like compared to a

34:53.860 --> 34:54.160
photograph.

34:54.860 --> 35:00.540
You figure it out by observing where the drawer's own internal structural rules force them to

35:00.540 --> 35:02.740
put a diagnostic mark later in the sequence.

35:03.300 --> 35:07.040
The spatial placement of one unit discloses the semantic reality of the others.

35:07.040 --> 35:07.640
Exactly.

35:08.180 --> 35:09.260
That is just incredible.

35:09.440 --> 35:11.520
It's like forensic accounting for art.

35:11.860 --> 35:16.260
And it brings us right to the heart of the essay, what the author conceptualizes as the

35:16.260 --> 35:17.520
continuation problem.

35:17.900 --> 35:19.920
The continuation problem is the core framework.

35:20.380 --> 35:24.840
This is where we stop thinking about drawing as making a picture and start thinking about

35:24.840 --> 35:28.080
it as playing a high stakes chess match against yourself.

35:28.340 --> 35:30.180
A very unforgiving chess match.

35:30.180 --> 35:33.960
Because when you are drawing with a pen or a marker, which is what these kids were using

35:33.960 --> 35:35.920
in the studies, marks are permanent.

35:36.440 --> 35:37.820
Erasure isn't an option.

35:38.400 --> 35:41.200
And the physical real estate on the paper is strictly limited.

35:41.880 --> 35:43.740
The borders of the page are absolute.

35:44.460 --> 35:48.440
So drawing isn't just about what you want to say about the dog or the house.

35:48.600 --> 35:53.640
It's heavily restricted by what the physical paper still allows you to say based on the

35:53.640 --> 35:55.460
irreversible moves you've already made.

35:55.820 --> 35:57.680
This introduces a critical concept.

35:57.680 --> 36:00.920
The essay formalizes as admissibility.

36:01.500 --> 36:01.980
Admissibility.

36:02.260 --> 36:02.480
Yes.

36:02.780 --> 36:07.900
At any given millisecond in the process of drawing, there is a specific, limited set

36:07.900 --> 36:12.140
of marks that your internal rule system will actually allow you to make next.

36:12.380 --> 36:13.660
Based on your syntax.

36:13.940 --> 36:14.320
Exactly.

36:14.980 --> 36:21.180
If your foundational rule is never cross a boundary line, then any potential mark that would require

36:21.180 --> 36:25.320
intersecting an existing line is instantly rendered inadmissible.

36:25.320 --> 36:28.680
It is removed from your cognitive menu of options.

36:29.140 --> 36:33.420
And every single time you put your pen to the paper and make a mark, you radically change

36:33.420 --> 36:35.180
that menu of options for your future self.

36:35.400 --> 36:38.240
You dynamically alter the admissible set.

36:38.620 --> 36:39.000
You do.

36:39.000 --> 36:44.540
And you do this in two primary ways, through licensing and through foreclosure.

36:45.220 --> 36:48.180
Let's define those mechanisms carefully because they are vital.

36:49.000 --> 36:54.480
A mark licenses when it creates a new boundary, a new structural attachment point, or a new

36:54.480 --> 36:56.960
enclosed region that later marks can utilize.

36:57.300 --> 36:58.160
Can you give an example?

36:58.600 --> 36:58.800
Sure.

36:59.360 --> 37:04.500
Drawing a large torso licenses the future addition of arms at the shoulders and legs at the base.

37:04.500 --> 37:07.620
It enables future communicative possibilities.

37:08.300 --> 37:09.360
It opens doors.

37:10.180 --> 37:10.200
Okay.

37:10.360 --> 37:11.100
And foreclosure?

37:12.360 --> 37:18.860
Conversely, a mark forecloses when it occupies physical space, closes off a contour, or introduces

37:18.860 --> 37:21.220
a boundary that later marks may not cross.

37:21.780 --> 37:24.240
It destroys future communicative possibilities.

37:24.780 --> 37:25.740
It closes doors.

37:25.740 --> 37:31.820
So if we go back to the train wheels experiment, when the kid decided to draw that extra structurally

37:31.820 --> 37:35.800
useless train carriage off the back, that was a pure licensing move.

37:36.160 --> 37:36.360
Yes.

37:36.860 --> 37:38.340
A massive licensing move.

37:38.620 --> 37:41.880
It added zero immediate diagnostic value to the picture.

37:42.060 --> 37:46.900
I mean, the train didn't suddenly look more like a realistic train just because it randomly

37:46.900 --> 37:47.740
had two cars.

37:47.900 --> 37:53.320
But it massively raised the reachable value of the entire drawing because it created a brand

37:53.320 --> 37:54.980
new empty underside.

37:55.800 --> 38:00.300
It literally licensed the legal placement of the new wheels without breaking the boundary

38:00.300 --> 38:00.640
rule.

38:01.060 --> 38:05.260
And on the other end of the spectrum, we have what the essay terms greedy foreclosure.

38:05.820 --> 38:07.360
Greedy foreclosure is a great term.

38:08.160 --> 38:12.260
Imagine a child wants to draw a complex scene, a person holding an umbrella.

38:12.620 --> 38:14.800
They start with a totally blank page.

38:15.280 --> 38:19.800
The most recognizable, highest value semantic feature of a person is usually the face and

38:19.800 --> 38:20.180
head, right?

38:20.460 --> 38:21.240
Usually, yes.

38:21.240 --> 38:27.660
So, operating greedily for immediate communicative value, they draw a massive, highly detailed

38:27.660 --> 38:29.820
head right dead in the center of the page.

38:30.040 --> 38:32.900
It has high immediate diagnostic value.

38:33.340 --> 38:35.300
But it's a massive strategic trap.

38:35.460 --> 38:36.120
Totally a trap.

38:36.300 --> 38:36.660
It is.

38:36.740 --> 38:37.840
A trap of their own making.

38:38.460 --> 38:43.400
Because the head is so large and so centrally located, it completely forecloses the physical

38:43.400 --> 38:46.560
possibility of drawing a proportionally sized torso beneath it.

38:46.560 --> 38:49.540
There is simply no paper left before hitting the bottom edge.

38:49.860 --> 38:50.640
They ran out of space.

38:51.360 --> 38:54.160
They can still attach small arms to the side of the giant head.

38:54.480 --> 38:58.920
But now, when they attempt to draw the umbrella the person is supposed to be holding, they are

38:58.920 --> 39:01.500
forced to draw it floating in the empty air beside the face.

39:01.620 --> 39:02.540
It just hovers there.

39:03.260 --> 39:08.480
What was meant to be a held object now looks like a speech bubble or a hat falling off.

39:09.000 --> 39:14.780
By chasing immediate semantic value, the ultimate reachable value of the intended scene plummeted.

39:14.780 --> 39:16.820
But it's like moving into a new apartment.

39:17.240 --> 39:19.880
You have a budget and you have limited floor space.

39:20.380 --> 39:25.080
If you go out and spend your entire budget on a massive, state-of-the-art 80-inch TV for

39:25.080 --> 39:27.040
the living room, that's greedy foreclosure.

39:27.280 --> 39:28.280
It feels great in the moment.

39:28.580 --> 39:28.840
Sure.

39:29.020 --> 39:30.580
The TV is awesome right now.

39:30.940 --> 39:32.720
It has high immediate value.

39:33.120 --> 39:35.820
But you spent all your money and took up an entire wall.

39:35.940 --> 39:37.600
So now you can't afford a couch to sit on.

39:37.880 --> 39:41.440
And even if you could, there's no room to put it at a comfortable viewing distance.

39:41.440 --> 39:45.900
You completely foreclosed on your future ability to actually enjoy the room.

39:46.660 --> 39:52.820
That tension between immediate payoff and future viability leads to a profound hypothesis in

39:52.820 --> 39:56.460
the essay about why children develop the specific drawing habits they do.

39:56.840 --> 39:57.960
The sequencing habits.

39:58.240 --> 39:58.420
Right.

39:59.420 --> 40:05.360
Good Now, along with her colleague Levine, documented incredibly consistent, almost universal

40:05.360 --> 40:08.960
sequencing regularities in how children draw and copy shapes.

40:09.460 --> 40:10.760
Cross-culturally too, right?

40:10.760 --> 40:11.440
Yes.

40:11.780 --> 40:15.840
Across cultures, children overwhelmingly prefer to start at the top of the page.

40:16.400 --> 40:18.120
They prefer to start on the left side.

40:18.800 --> 40:23.940
They prefer to draw enclosing contours, the outer outlines of an object, before they draw

40:23.940 --> 40:25.360
any of the internal details.

40:26.020 --> 40:30.220
And they prefer to anchor new strokes to existing ones rather than leaving them floating.

40:30.380 --> 40:34.360
Now, a skeptic might just say, well, in Western cultures, we read from left to right,

40:34.500 --> 40:35.080
top to bottom.

40:35.240 --> 40:38.040
So they just draw that way because of early handwriting habits.

40:38.180 --> 40:39.100
That's the easy answer.

40:39.100 --> 40:39.540
Right.

40:39.720 --> 40:45.580
But the essay suggests something much deeper and more structural than just mimicking reading.

40:46.360 --> 40:51.180
What if these sequencing regularities aren't just cultural habits but are actually universal

40:51.180 --> 40:53.260
option preservation strategies?

40:53.260 --> 40:58.880
What's fascinating here is the underlying functional logic behind those physical habits.

40:59.540 --> 41:01.400
Think about the geometry of the blank page.

41:01.600 --> 41:08.900
Starting at the very top left preserves the absolute largest contiguous region of free space down and to the right.

41:08.900 --> 41:12.140
For whatever marks you might discover you need to make later.

41:12.540 --> 41:14.040
You're maximizing future options.

41:14.480 --> 41:20.740
Drawing the outer contour first establishes and fixes the boundaries that all the interior details must respect

41:20.740 --> 41:23.760
before you commit to placing those interior details.

41:24.160 --> 41:25.100
You're setting the borders.

41:25.680 --> 41:27.800
These aren't merely trained motor habits.

41:28.320 --> 41:30.600
They are low-cost cognitive heuristics.

41:30.600 --> 41:36.660
They are mental shortcuts designed specifically to avoid the trap of greedy foreclosure.

41:37.340 --> 41:41.020
They are strategies for keeping your spatial options open for as long as possible.

41:41.020 --> 41:45.980
Which means the person drawing is constantly negotiating a high wire act.

41:46.340 --> 41:51.540
They are trying to satisfy fans' outward direction, making the drawing discriminable and recognizable

41:51.540 --> 41:57.000
for the viewer by including key features, while simultaneously satisfying Goodnow's backward direction,

41:57.560 --> 42:02.500
choosing a sequence of marks that remains physically constructible and doesn't paint them into a literal corner.

42:02.500 --> 42:07.480
And this brings us to a critical methodological warning for anyone studying cognition.

42:08.200 --> 42:10.440
We might term it the endpoint illusion.

42:10.780 --> 42:12.120
The endpoint illusion.

42:12.700 --> 42:16.220
Because the process of negotiation is so complex,

42:16.680 --> 42:20.500
and because marks accumulate in such a highly specific dependent order,

42:21.140 --> 42:26.980
if we only evaluate the final finished product, we are almost guaranteed to be fooled.

42:26.980 --> 42:33.200
Because the entire grueling process of spatial negotiation is completely invisible to endpoint measures.

42:33.780 --> 42:38.040
Two totally different kids could produce the exact same wobbly stick figure on a page,

42:38.140 --> 42:41.540
but they might have taken completely different cognitive paths to get there.

42:41.700 --> 42:42.160
Exactly.

42:42.400 --> 42:47.520
One kid might have a rigid, highly stable practice procedure they've executed a hundred times.

42:48.160 --> 42:51.280
Another kid might have just stumbled into the final shape by accident,

42:51.580 --> 42:55.000
narrowly avoiding spatial disasters and rule violations along the way.

42:55.000 --> 42:58.580
If you just grade the final picture, you give them both a gold star,

42:58.740 --> 43:02.120
but you've learned absolutely nothing about their actual cognitive organization.

43:03.080 --> 43:05.980
And this endpoint illusion doesn't just fool human psychologists.

43:06.640 --> 43:09.800
It currently fools state-of-the-art artificial intelligence.

43:10.260 --> 43:12.120
Really? AI falls for this too.

43:12.360 --> 43:13.100
Oh, absolutely.

43:13.440 --> 43:18.300
The essay discusses modern AI vision benchmarks like SEVA and Physion,

43:18.820 --> 43:23.900
which are designed to test how well AI models interpret and predict physical scenes and human sketches.

43:23.900 --> 43:29.460
I mean, we hear so much about how AI vision models are getting incredibly good at looking at a quick sketch

43:29.460 --> 43:32.980
and instantly saying, yes, that is a dog, or that is an airplane.

43:33.360 --> 43:36.880
They score extremely high accuracy rates on these recognition benchmarks.

43:37.320 --> 43:39.320
They do recognize image as well.

43:39.700 --> 43:41.800
But the paper points out a massive blind spot.

43:42.140 --> 43:46.720
How exactly does the AI fail when it's forced to actually engage with the drawing process?

43:47.440 --> 43:49.240
Okay, so what happens?

43:49.240 --> 43:53.700
The failure becomes apparent when researchers force the AI to produce a sketch

43:53.700 --> 43:56.220
or to perform a task called sparsification.

43:57.660 --> 43:58.140
Sparsification.

43:59.100 --> 44:03.220
Sparsification is when you take a detailed image and ask the system to simplify it down

44:03.220 --> 44:05.600
to just its most essential communicative strokes,

44:05.980 --> 44:09.380
usually under a strict artificial limit on time or virtual ink.

44:09.980 --> 44:12.740
Like drawing a caricature with only five lines.

44:13.320 --> 44:13.580
Right.

44:13.580 --> 44:18.420
When AI models attempt this, they fail to sparsify the image the way a human would,

44:18.920 --> 44:22.480
and they produce reliably distinct non-human error patterns.

44:23.140 --> 44:26.020
Wait, what does an AI error pattern look like in this context?

44:26.280 --> 44:29.940
If it knows what a dog looks like, why can't it just draw a simple dog?

44:30.040 --> 44:34.920
Because merely recognizing a completed dog sketch does not mean the convolutional neural network

44:34.920 --> 44:37.420
possesses a human-like abstraction policy.

44:37.420 --> 44:42.340
It doesn't actually represent which specific structural marks are expendable to a human mind

44:42.340 --> 44:43.700
and which are vital.

44:44.200 --> 44:44.800
Ah, I see.

44:45.380 --> 44:48.360
The AI might be successfully guessing the category of dog

44:48.360 --> 44:51.960
based purely on aggregate pixel patterns and statistical textures.

44:52.800 --> 44:55.360
But a human child, even when drawing quickly,

44:55.480 --> 44:59.080
is running a complex algorithmic simulation of boundary rules,

44:59.560 --> 45:01.560
licensing moves, and audience anticipation.

45:02.240 --> 45:04.740
The AI lacks the procedural syntax entirely.

45:04.740 --> 45:09.180
So, if a kid and an AI both output a simple stick figure,

45:09.740 --> 45:14.660
the AI basically guessed the answer based on a massive math equation of pixel probabilities,

45:14.900 --> 45:19.760
but the kid navigated a cognitive obstacle course of rules and constraints.

45:20.800 --> 45:23.700
And when we only test AI for recognizability,

45:24.120 --> 45:28.180
we are essentially telling the AI that the human's non-referential organization,

45:28.420 --> 45:30.820
the syntax, is just useless noise.

45:31.280 --> 45:33.420
Consider the mandalas we discussed earlier.

45:33.420 --> 45:36.860
A child draws a beautifully balanced, symmetrical mandala

45:36.860 --> 45:39.140
not because it refers to anything in the real world,

45:39.260 --> 45:42.340
but purely for the internal joy of the constructional achievement.

45:42.520 --> 45:43.340
It's just satisfying.

45:43.720 --> 45:46.680
It's about balance, rhythm, and mastering a closed shape.

45:47.320 --> 45:51.180
Later, they might repurpose that mandala to serve as a torso in a drawing of a person.

45:52.000 --> 45:55.640
If an AI evaluates that drawing solely on its ultimate recognizability,

45:56.300 --> 46:00.500
it credits the mandala only insofar as it successfully mimics a human torso.

46:01.020 --> 46:02.260
It misses the point entirely.

46:02.260 --> 46:07.660
It completely misses the profound fact that the human selected the form for its aesthetic balance first

46:07.660 --> 46:09.760
and assigned the semantic meaning second.

46:10.440 --> 46:13.060
It totally erases the human element of the creation.

46:13.660 --> 46:17.040
So if we can't trust the final image because of the endpoint illusion,

46:17.460 --> 46:20.900
and we clearly can't trust the AI's grading system because it only looks at pixels,

46:21.100 --> 46:26.060
how do researchers actually measure this incredible, invisible cognitive process today?

46:26.060 --> 46:32.280
The essay proposes that we must build experimental measures that reach into the timeline of the process itself.

46:32.760 --> 46:36.400
We have to treat the drawing as a temporal event, not a static object.

46:36.800 --> 46:37.540
How do they do that?

46:37.920 --> 46:41.820
One method is through budget ladders, which FAN's research program utilizes.

46:42.460 --> 46:45.320
Instead of asking a participant for one single drawing,

46:45.720 --> 46:48.880
you ask them for a family of drawings of the same object,

46:49.120 --> 46:52.240
but under increasingly tight budgets of time or ink.

46:52.240 --> 46:53.480
Like a speed round.

46:53.980 --> 46:57.700
Right. You might give them 30 seconds, then 10 seconds, then 2 seconds.

46:58.260 --> 47:03.600
What survives at the absolute tightest budget reveals the drawer's highest cognitive priority.

47:04.080 --> 47:08.960
And we can also bring back GoodNow's inherited figure probes, but update them with modern tech.

47:09.240 --> 47:15.300
The essay mentions a recent tablet-based study by researchers Philipson, Tsuji, and Nagai that did this brilliantly.

47:15.300 --> 47:17.200
Yes, the tablet study.

47:17.660 --> 47:22.740
They set up a tablet at a museum where a drawing was only partially finished and let kids complete it,

47:22.820 --> 47:27.560
but they structured the experiment with three very different starting conditions to test the kids' syntax.

47:28.040 --> 47:30.980
The design of those conditions is highly revealing.

47:31.540 --> 47:34.360
In the first condition, the children were given just the outline,

47:34.660 --> 47:38.580
the enclosing boundary contour of a face, a house, or a car.

47:38.980 --> 47:42.200
Which aligns perfectly with GoodNow's favored natural sequence.

47:42.200 --> 47:48.680
It provides the contour up front, safely licensing the addition of inner details without risk of greedy foreclosure.

47:49.160 --> 47:49.720
Exactly.

47:50.260 --> 47:53.100
Now, in the second condition, they reversed the natural order.

47:53.700 --> 47:57.680
They gave the children only the inner features, eyes floating in empty space,

47:58.080 --> 48:00.200
or windows without a house surrounding them.

48:00.300 --> 48:00.940
Oh, that's hard.

48:01.680 --> 48:07.320
It forces the child to recognize the parts and attempt to supply a contour that successfully encloses them,

48:07.960 --> 48:11.000
posing the embracing line problem from the outside in.

48:11.000 --> 48:14.740
And the third condition was absolutely diabolical.

48:15.140 --> 48:16.600
They scrambled the inner features.

48:16.860 --> 48:20.760
So, on the tablet, you have an eye down in the corner, a nose up at the top,

48:21.060 --> 48:23.340
a window floating randomly off to the side.

48:23.520 --> 48:26.980
The child's internal spatial rules are totally shattered from the very start.

48:27.300 --> 48:30.320
They are faced with an impossible spatial environment.

48:30.700 --> 48:35.940
They either have to invent a completely novel, highly complex, admissible figure to integrate this mess,

48:36.040 --> 48:38.780
or just give up and scribble over the whole thing in frustration.

48:38.780 --> 48:43.800
And crucially, the researchers didn't just score the final product for accuracy or realism.

48:44.380 --> 48:50.420
They tracked the tablet's data to score the relationship between the child's new digital marks and the inherited marks.

48:50.580 --> 48:52.400
Like the actual digital footprint.

48:52.660 --> 48:52.860
Right.

48:53.320 --> 48:54.880
Did they meticulously trace?

48:55.220 --> 48:56.660
Did they color inside the lines?

48:57.100 --> 48:58.880
Did they connect the disjointed parts?

48:59.520 --> 49:00.480
Did they just scribble?

49:00.480 --> 49:05.960
Well, this experimental design brilliantly uses fans' modern digital measurement techniques,

49:06.080 --> 49:09.500
but answers GoodNow's fundamental structural question.

49:10.240 --> 49:14.400
How does a mind continue a spatial state it did not originally create?

49:14.880 --> 49:20.560
So, what does this all mean for us, for the adults listening to this deep dive who haven't drawn a stick figure in 20 years?

49:20.560 --> 49:24.960
Because this cognitive machinery doesn't just evaporate when we grow up.

49:25.440 --> 49:26.460
It definitely doesn't.

49:26.760 --> 49:32.540
The essay takes all of this, GoodNow's backward spatial constraints and fans' outward audience communication,

49:32.780 --> 49:37.780
and applies it directly to the adult world of diagrams, graphs, and data visualizations.

49:38.160 --> 49:44.920
If we connect this to the bigger picture, a corporate data visualization is fundamentally just a highly formalized drawing.

49:44.920 --> 49:50.840
The cognitive scientist Barbara Tversky has extensively shown how spatial layout conventions,

49:51.120 --> 49:59.220
like the idea that up means more or better, left to right indicates time passing, and lines denote causal connection,

49:59.880 --> 50:05.800
actually recruit our basic physical spatial reasoning to process abstract conceptual font.

50:06.020 --> 50:07.360
We use space to think.

50:07.360 --> 50:17.180
But, the adult designer of a data visualization faces the exact same continuation problem as the five-year-old child trying to add wheels to a train.

50:17.340 --> 50:17.600
Yes.

50:18.040 --> 50:22.600
Think about the last time you were prepping a slide deck or a data viz for a crucial meeting at work.

50:23.060 --> 50:26.260
You aren't just neutrally slapping numbers onto a grid.

50:26.740 --> 50:32.040
When you open your software and choose to make a scatter plot, the very first thing you do is to find your X and Y axes.

50:32.360 --> 50:33.600
Which is a massive commitment.

50:33.600 --> 50:36.740
That is the structural equivalent of drawing the giant head first.

50:37.360 --> 50:44.400
Choosing those specific axes immediately and permanently forecloses your ability to easily show certain other relationships in the data

50:44.400 --> 50:47.120
before you've even plotted a single data point.

50:47.300 --> 50:48.340
The constraints are identical.

50:48.860 --> 50:53.820
If you are drawing a schematic diagram of a complex workflow on a whiteboard,

50:54.400 --> 50:58.820
and you boldly place the most important department node directly in the center of the board,

50:59.420 --> 51:01.940
you are making a massive licensing move.

51:02.020 --> 51:02.760
You're anchoring it.

51:02.760 --> 51:05.100
You are licensing radial connections.

51:05.520 --> 51:09.400
You can draw arrows pointing in and out from all 360 degrees.

51:09.800 --> 51:12.700
But by permanently occupying that center space,

51:12.920 --> 51:20.720
you have simultaneously severely compromised your ability to draw a long, linear, step-by-step causal chain across the board.

51:20.880 --> 51:21.940
You blocked your own path.

51:21.940 --> 51:29.320
Every single mark you make on that whiteboard, or that slide deck, carries both the outward communicative intention,

51:29.920 --> 51:32.160
trying to get your boss to understand the quarterly data,

51:32.620 --> 51:37.880
and the backward indexical residue of the spatial choices and compromises you were forced to make.

51:37.980 --> 51:39.660
It's the exact same tug-of-war.

51:39.660 --> 51:44.820
You desperately want the chart to be understandable and impactful to the audience, which is fans' domain,

51:44.820 --> 51:49.920
but you also have to physically fit all the boxes, legends, and lines onto one single slide

51:49.920 --> 51:54.000
without it looking like an incomprehensible bowl of spaghetti, which is good-nosed domain.

51:54.300 --> 51:58.680
A diagram is, at its core, an icon of relations.

51:58.680 --> 52:04.740
And a theory of data or art that only looks at what the diagram is supposed to mean semantically

52:04.740 --> 52:08.180
will mistakenly attribute structures to the data itself

52:08.180 --> 52:12.560
that actually just belong to the designer's spatial problem-solving procedure.

52:13.460 --> 52:17.300
We mistake the limitations of the page for the reality of the data.

52:17.840 --> 52:18.580
Perfectly said.

52:19.320 --> 52:22.220
We have covered an immense amount of ground today.

52:22.320 --> 52:25.480
We started with a kindergartner's wobbly stick figure

52:25.480 --> 52:29.380
and ended up analyzing the spatial physics of corporate data visualizations.

52:30.140 --> 52:34.020
But the core truth remains beautifully consistent across all of it.

52:34.300 --> 52:35.000
It really does.

52:35.700 --> 52:40.980
Drawing, whether it's crayons or software, is not a defective attempt at photographic realism.

52:41.400 --> 52:46.540
It is a highly evolved, deeply sophisticated technology for goal-sensitive compression.

52:47.040 --> 52:50.460
It is ruled by a strict, invisible spatial syntax

52:50.460 --> 52:53.640
and a keen, calculating awareness of the audience.

52:53.640 --> 52:59.240
The next time you watch a child draw a bird with human arms or a person with banana figures,

52:59.920 --> 53:02.240
you are not watching a failure of perception.

53:02.880 --> 53:06.060
You are witnessing live, visible cognitive processing.

53:06.560 --> 53:10.160
You are watching a mind actively negotiating space and meaning,

53:10.780 --> 53:16.020
mark by permanent mark, solving complex spatial crises in real time.

53:16.020 --> 53:19.660
It is truly a chess match against a blank page.

53:20.240 --> 53:23.860
Which leaves us with one final thought for you to take away from this deep dive.

53:24.520 --> 53:28.520
Tomorrow, or perhaps later today, when you are trapped on a long phone call

53:28.520 --> 53:29.720
or sitting in a boring meeting,

53:30.180 --> 53:33.920
you will inevitably start mindlessly doodling on a notepad in front of you.

53:34.060 --> 53:34.740
We all do it.

53:35.040 --> 53:38.680
When you do, I want you to stop and actually look closely at your pen.

53:39.340 --> 53:40.100
Where did you start?

53:40.580 --> 53:42.340
Are you starting at the top left corner?

53:42.340 --> 53:46.100
Are you drawing thick, heavy borders before you carefully shade them in?

53:46.580 --> 53:48.480
When you think you aren't thinking at all,

53:48.780 --> 53:53.840
what invisible, deep-seated geometric rules is your brain strictly enforcing on that page?

53:54.320 --> 53:58.140
You might be surprised to discover the hidden architecture of your own mind,

53:58.480 --> 53:59.320
hiding in plain sight.

